=[ Tomet CMS ]

Content management system for the Tomet ecosystem.

==[ Overview ]

`tomet-cms` is the UI-less, I/O-free content management engine for the Tomet ecosystem, designed to power browser-based editing (e.g. in `tomet-wiki`). It provides document synchronization, three-way AST merging, and conflict resolution over a remote Git host without requiring a local clone.

===[ What it is ]

-| **UI-less & I/O-free library**: Pure logic engine compiled for both native and WebAssembly (`wasm32-unknown-unknown`).
-| **AST-level 3-way merge**: Resolves concurrent document edits using `tomet::merge::merge` instead of line-based diff3.
-| **Optimistic save flow**: Saves directly against the remote head revision with automatic, bounded retries on branch movement.
-| **Byte-identical conflict resolution**: Resolves individual `@conflict` markers by span substitution without re-formatting the surrounding document.

===[ What it is not ]

-| Not a CLI or application: No binary entry points or terminal interface.
-| Not a UI framework: Presentation and user interaction live downstream in `tomet-wiki`.
-| Not an HTTP client: All remote interactions are abstracted through the async `Host` trait; callers supply transport and authentication.

===[ Scope ]

-| **One save = one file = one commit**: Multi-file commits and page renaming/moving are deferred.
-| **`.tmt` documents only**: Non-`.tmt` paths are rejected with a typed error (`SaveError::NonTmtPath`); attachment policy is out of scope.
-| **Host-agnostic core**: GitHub is the first target implementation, but `tomet-cms` (core) contains no host-specific assumptions.

==[ Save Flow ]

1. Caller submits a `SaveRequest`: either `SaveRequest::create` for a fresh document or `SaveRequest::edit` with a known `base` revision. Every request specifies a commit message.
2. At save time, the engine fetches the target branch's current `head` revision and retrieves `remote` content at `head`.
3. If creating (`base_rev: None`) and the file already exists remotely, the save fails immediately with `SaveError::AlreadyExists` and commits nothing (no merge).
4. If editing from a base revision:
   - If `remote == base`, the fast path commits `local` content directly.
   - If remote has changed, AST three-way merge is computed (`base`/`local`/`remote`):
     - Non-overlapping edits merge without conflict markers (`has_conflicts: false`).
     - Conflicting edits record `@conflict(a: ..., b: ...)` markers in-place (`has_conflicts: true`) and commit the resulting file as normal.
     - Remote deletions with local modifications follow Git conventions: local content is preserved and reported with a provisional `SaveWarning::ModifyDeleteConflict`.
5. The committed text is inspected via the `conflict` module to determine `has_conflicts: bool`.
6. The result is committed on top of `head` with the caller-supplied commit message. If the branch moved concurrently, the engine retries up to a bounded limit.

==[ WebAssembly Constraints ]

-| Compiles cleanly to `wasm32-unknown-unknown`.
-| Contains no `std::fs`, `std::net`, `std::time::SystemTime`, or threading dependencies in `core` or `github`.
-| The `Host` and `HttpClient` traits use asynchronous methods returning `!Send` futures, as browser wasm runtimes are single-threaded.

==[ GitHub Host (`tomet-cms-github`) ]

`tomet-cms-github` provides a GitHub GraphQL implementation of `tomet-cms::Host` (`GitHubHost`) suitable for both native and WebAssembly (`wasm32-unknown-unknown`) environments.

===[ Design Principles ]

-| **Zero I/O & Injected Transport**: The crate performs no network calls, opens no sockets, and brings in no HTTP runtime dependencies. Callers inject an implementation of the `HttpClient` trait.
-| **Pure WebAssembly Compatibility**: Only relies on `serde`, `serde_json`, and `base64` with `alloc` feature enabled (no `std` I/O). Fully operational under `wasm32-unknown-unknown`.
-| **Single GraphQL Endpoint**: All operations (`get_head`, `read_file`, `commit_file`) execute against `https://api.github.com/graphql` via strongly-typed GraphQL request/response structures (`repository`, `object`, and `createCommitOnBranch`).
-| **Credential Redaction**: `GitHubHost`'s custom `fmt::Debug` implementation always prints `token: "[REDACTED]"`. Authentication tokens (such as GitHub Personal Access Tokens) are never leaked in debug dumps, log messages, or error strings.
-| **Robust Branch Movement Detection**: If a commit mutation fails, `GitHubHost` queries the latest branch HEAD revision. If the actual HEAD differs from the caller's expected HEAD, it returns `HostError::BranchMoved`, triggering `tomet-cms`'s automatic retry flow. If HEAD is unchanged, the original GraphQL error (e.g. permission or validation error) is preserved and returned.

===[ `HttpClient` Trait Contract ]

The transport abstraction consists of `HttpRequest`, `HttpResponse`, `HttpError`, and `HttpClient`:

```rust
pub trait HttpClient {
    async fn send(&self, request: HttpRequest) -> Result<HttpResponse, HttpError>;
}
```

-| **`HttpRequest`**: Contains `method` (`HttpMethod`), `url`, `headers` (`Vec<(String, String)>`), and `body` (`Vec<u8>`).
-| **`HttpResponse`**: Contains HTTP `status` code (`u16`), `headers`, and `body` (`Vec<u8>`).
-| **Single-threaded Wasm**: Futures are not required to be `Send`, making them directly usable in single-threaded WebAssembly runtimes.

===[ JavaScript `fetch` Integration ]

In JavaScript/WebAssembly environments (`bindings/js`), `JsHttpClient` implements `HttpClient` by delegating to `globalThis.fetch` (or a caller-supplied `fetch` function) via `js-sys` and `wasm-bindgen-futures`, without assuming a browser `window` global.

===[ Offline & Fake Client ]

For unit tests and offline testing, `tomet-cms-github` provides `FakeHttpClient`:

```rust
let client = FakeHttpClient::new();
client.queue_response(HttpResponse::new(200, b"{\"data\": ...}".to_vec()));
let host = GitHubHost::new(client.clone(), "owner", "repo", "main", "dummy-token");
```

==[ JavaScript & WebAssembly Bindings (`@tomet/cms`) ]

`bindings/js` provides the `@tomet/cms` npm package, exposing `tomet-cms` and `tomet-cms-github` to JavaScript and TypeScript runtimes (browsers, Node.js, Deno, Cloudflare Workers) via `wasm-bindgen`.

===[ Usage ]

```javascript
import init, { GitHubCms, listConflicts, resolveConflict } from '@tomet/cms';

// Initialize the WebAssembly module
await init();

// Synchronous conflict inspection & single-conflict resolution
const conflicts = listConflicts(sourceText);
const resolved = resolveConflict(sourceText, 0, 'a');

// GitHub CMS client
const cms = new GitHubCms({
  owner: 'octocat',
  repo: 'my-wiki',
  branch: 'main', // optional, defaults to "main"
  token: 'github_pat_...',
});

// Create fresh document
const createResult = await cms.createFile({
  path: 'notes/welcome.tmt',
  content: '=[ Welcome ]\n\nHello, world!\n',
  message: 'docs: create welcome page',
});

// Edit existing document with AST 3-way merge & automatic retry
const editResult = await cms.editFile({
  path: 'notes/welcome.tmt',
  baseRev: createResult.revision,
  content: '=[ Welcome ]\n\nUpdated content.\n',
  message: 'docs: update welcome page',
});
```

===[ Machine-Readable Errors (`.code`) ]

All errors thrown or rejected across the Wasm boundary are standard JavaScript `Error` instances containing a machine-readable `.code: string` property:

-| **`SaveError`**: `"NonTmtPath"`, `"AlreadyExists"`, `"MaxRetriesExceeded"`, `"ParseError"`
-| **`HostError`**: `"Unauthorized"`, `"Forbidden"`, `"RateLimited"`, `"NonTextBlob"`, `"BranchMoved"`, `"RevisionNotFound"`, `"HostOther"`
-| **`ConflictError`**: `"ConflictParseError"`, `"IndexOutOfBounds"`, `"InvalidSide"`

===[ Custom Transport & Offline Testing ]

Callers can pass a custom `fetch`-compatible function in `new GitHubCms({ ..., fetch })` to intercept requests, mock responses, or run completely offline without real network access.

==[ Workspace Layout ]

-| @dir(crates/core) (`tomet-cms`) -- Core library.
-| @dir(crates/github) (`tomet-cms-github`) -- GitHub host implementation over GraphQL.
-| @dir(bindings/js) (`tomet-cms-js` / `@tomet/cms`) -- WebAssembly bindings and JS/TS SDK.

==[ Building and Testing ]

=== Build workspace

```bash
just build
```

=== Run tests

```bash
just test        # Rust unit and integration tests
just test-wasm   # Node.js Wasm integration test suite
```

=== Check code

```bash
just check
just clippy
just check-wasm  # check WebAssembly compilation across crates
just build-wasm  # build release Wasm binary and pkg/ bindings
```

=== Documentation

```bash
just docs        # regenerate Markdown from tmtroot/
just docs-check  # verify docs match exports
```
